  <section id="details">
    <h2>About this page</h2>
    <p>
      This page sets out the route by which many Circassians reached Syria:
      from the Caucasus, through the Ottoman Balkans, to the Golan Heights. It
      uses six published studies of how Circassian refugees were settled in
      Ottoman Syria. It then sets the grandfather’s account from the
      biographies already on this site beside that route.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How claims are backed.</strong> Every claim of fact is followed by
      a citation in square brackets: the study and the page it comes from, which
      links to the full entry under <a href="#sources">Sources</a>, or a research
      page on this site. A claim with no citation is my own comment, and I say
      so. Page numbers are the ones printed in each work.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Compiled</dt>
      <dd><time datetime="2026-11-01">1 November 2026</time></dd>
      <dt>Sources</dt>
      <dd>Six studies, listed with their dates in <a href="#sources">Sources</a></dd>
      <dt>Languages</dt>
      <dd>
        English, Russian and Arabic. The Russian and Arabic passages are my own
        translations, made with an AI assistant. See
        <a href="/more-info/">More info</a>.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: <strong>none of the six studies names Amin Samkough’s family.</strong>
        I searched the text of each one for the name in its English, Russian,
        Turkish and Arabic spellings and found nothing. (The 2017 article by
        Akbulat does name “Amin Samgug”, but only in a later passage about his
        appeals to the League of Nations, not about migration
        <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 9" />.) So this page documents
        the <em>general</em> history of the route, which the studies agree on,
        and sets the grandfather’s account beside it. It does not prove that his
        family took this route.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="route">
    <h2>The route in short</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Circassia</strong>, the north-west Caucasus. Russian conquest
        drove out a million or more people, with the peak in 1864
        <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 8" />
        <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 5" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Ottoman Balkans, 1860s.</strong> The Ottoman state settled
        many of them in the Balkans (now Bulgaria, Serbia, Bosnia, Romania,
        Greece and Kosovo), then called Rumelia
        <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 6" />
        <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 202" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Renewed displacement, 1877&ndash;78.</strong> After the
        Russo-Ottoman war the Ottomans had to move the Circassians out of the
        Balkans <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" />
        <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 6" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Syrian ports, 1878.</strong> Ships landed tens of thousands
        at Beirut, Acre, Haifa, Latakia and other ports
        <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" />
        <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 10" />. Others came overland
        <Cite src="lash" at="web page" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Hauran, Quneitra and the Golan.</strong> Part of the
        Balkan Circassians were sent to the Golan, in the Hauran sanjak
        <Cite src="mirzoeva" at="web text" />
        <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 10" />. The Golan became the largest
        Circassian area in Syria, and Mansura was one of its villages
        <Cite src="lash" at="web page" />.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="step-1">
    <h2>1. From Circassia to the Ottoman Balkans (1860s)</h2>
    <p>
      The studies agree that this was a first exile. The Sabancı thesis opens
      by saying that the 1878 move was a second one for these people:
    </p>
    <blockquote lang="en">
      “For Circassians, this was a re-experience of what happened nearly twenty
      years earlier. They were exiled from Russia, and they settled in Rumelia
      by Ottoman authorities.” <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 1" />
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Akbulat dates the first wave of migration from the Caucasus to 1859, with
      the peak after 1862 <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 5" />. He says the
      Ottomans settled most of them in Western Thrace, Western Anatolia and the
      Balkans, and gives more than 300,000 as settling in the European parts of
      the empire <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 6" />. Kazzaz puts the peak of
      migration to the Ottoman Empire in 1864 <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 8" />,
      and Akbulat names 21 May 1864 as the day Russia ended the “Caucasian
      wars” <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 5" />. Chochiev says that in the 1860s
      the main flow went to the Balkans and Anatolia, and that about
      175,000&ndash;200,000 Circassians had been settled in the Balkans by the
      time of the war of 1877 <Cite src="chochiev" at="pp. 202&ndash;203" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: in his introduction Akbulat says Syrian Circassians “in the 17th
        century” were first settled in the Balkans
        <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 3" />. The rest of his text, and every other
        source here, puts this in the 1860s. I take “17th century” to be a slip
        for the 19th (this is my reading), but I could not check it against any
        other copy.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="step-2">
    <h2>2. Renewed displacement after 1877&ndash;78</h2>
    <p>
      The war of 1877&ndash;78 ended in an Ottoman defeat. Chochiev writes that
      at the peace talks in San Stefano and Berlin the Ottomans had to agree to
      Russia’s demand to remove the Circassians settled in the Balkans in the
      1860s, who were blamed for violence against the local Christian
      population <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" />. Akbulat likewise says the
      Berlin agreement led to the eviction of the Circassians from the Balkans
      <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 6" />. Mirzoeva and Apazheva add that the
      Sultan issued a decree on 18 April 1879 forbidding Circassians to return
      to the European part of the empire <Cite src="mirzoeva" at="web text" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before 1878 only small groups had been sent to Syria. Kazzaz says the
      first settlements there date from the 1870s, and that a “dramatic
      increase” came from 1878 <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 9" />. ORSAM says the
      real migration wave to Syria began after the war of 1877&ndash;78, mainly
      from the Balkan territories <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 9" />.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="step-3">
    <h2>3. To the Syrian ports (1878)</h2>
    <p>
      Chochiev, citing Ottoman and French sources, gives the timing and the
      ports:
    </p>
    <blockquote lang="en">
      From mid-February to mid-April 1878, about 25,000 Circassians were
      brought on Ottoman and foreign ships to Beirut, Haifa, Acre, Jaffa,
      Latakia, Iskenderun and other ports of Syria, Lebanon and Palestine.
      <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" />
      <br />(My translation from the Russian.)
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The Arabic site <em>Syrian Contemporary History</em> puts it more
      broadly:
    </p>
    <blockquote lang="en">
      In 1878, tens of thousands of displaced Circassians poured into Greater
      Syria, coming from the Balkans (by sea and by land) and from the
      Anatolian peninsula (by land).
      <Cite src="lash" at="opening paragraph" />
      <br />(My translation from the Arabic.)
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Akbulat and Kazzaz both print a total of <strong>26,182</strong>
      Circassians arriving at ports in the Levant between February and
      September 1878, from a book by Berzeg
      <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 7, Table 1" />
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 13" />. Akbulat calls the exact figure hard to
      determine and says most of the migrants died during and after settlement
      <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 7" />. ORSAM says thousands died of
      starvation and disease after landing <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 10" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: <strong>the 26,182 figure.</strong> The ten rows of Akbulat’s
        table (Beirut, Acre, Tripoli, Latakia twice, Hama/Homs, Tripoli again,
        Haifa, Acre again and Beirut again) add up to 27,382, not 26,182
        <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 7, Table 1" />. The
        two differ by exactly one 1,200-person row. The table also lists
        Latakia twice with the same 2,500, and lists Hama/Homs, which is
        inland, as a port. Kazzaz dates the 10,000 sent to Hama to July
        <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 13" />, while
        Akbulat’s table dates it to 4 March. I have not seen Berzeg’s book, so
        I cannot tell which is right. Treat 26,182 as “about 25,000 to 27,000”
        (my reading).
      </mark>
    </p>
    <p>
      Kazzaz also describes the arrival itself. A group meant for Acre was left
      on the coast for four days, forced open a locked mosque, and a fight
      followed. After about twenty years in Rumelia, he writes, they “endured
      similar traumas” in Syria <Cite src="kazzaz" at="pp. 12&ndash;13" />.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="step-4">
    <h2>4. Into the Hauran, Quneitra and the Golan</h2>
    <p>
      From the ports the refugees were sent inland. The Ottoman plan of July
      1878, as Chochiev reports it, was to send 25,000 of those moved from the
      Balkans to the Damascus province and 50,000 to the Aleppo province. That
      was a plan, not a count of who arrived
      <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" />. (Kazzaz dates a similar plan, for
      25,000 to Damascus, to August 1878 <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 13" />.)
    </p>
    <p>
      Three of the studies say that Balkan Circassians went to the Golan in
      particular:
    </p>
    <blockquote lang="ru">
      Другую часть черкесов, переселенных с балканских земель, направили на
      Голанские высоты, в санджак Хауран.
      <br />
      “Another part of the Circassians resettled from the Balkan lands were sent
      to the Golan Heights, to the sanjak of Hauran.”
      <Cite src="mirzoeva" at="web text" /> (my translation)
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote lang="en">
      “… another group coming from Balkans were settled in the Golan Heights in
      the Sanjak of Hauran.” <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 10" />
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The third is Kazzaz, who says that after the tragic losses a group of
      Circassians “dispatched to the semi-arid lands of Hawran”, and that the
      district of Quneitra was “a principal area” of their settlement
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 1" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      ORSAM adds that the Circassians were “mainly settled in the Golan Heights
      as a military line”, set between the Druze areas and the Bedouin tribes
      <Cite src="orsam" at="pp. 10&ndash;11" />. Chochiev gives the same
      reason: the Quneitra colonies were founded to offset Druze opposition to
      the government <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" />. Kazzaz says that
      Circassians “built or expanded the population” of Quneitra,
      <strong>Mansura</strong>, Ayn Zivan, Mumsiya, Cuveyza, Breyka, Bir Acam
      and Surman <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 31" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Arabic article by Lash lists the Golan villages in the same way, with
      Mansura first among the named villages, and calls the Golan the largest
      Circassian concentration in Syria <Cite src="lash" at="web page" />:
    </p>
    <blockquote lang="ar" dir="rtl">
      …القنيطرة، وقرى: المنصورة، وعين زيوان، والمومسية، والجويزة …
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      That is, “… Quneitra, and the villages of: al-Mansura, Ayn Zivan,
      al-Mumsiya, al-Juwayza …” (my translation of an extract).
    </p>

    <h3 id="before-1878">Settlement before 1878</h3>
    <p>
      Some Circassians reached the Golan earlier, so the Balkan route was not the
      only one. Counts differ: about 400 in Quneitra in the early 1870s
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 31" />, 400 each near Homs, in the Hauran and on
      the Golan <Cite src="mirzoeva" at="web text" />, about 1,000 across Hama,
      Homs and the Golan in 1872
      <Cite src="orsam" at="pp. 8&ndash;9" />
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 9" />, and about 1,500 in two colonies near Homs
      and Quneitra <Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 202" />. ORSAM adds an oral
      account from elders of a different route for these early groups: by ship
      to Samsun, then to Uzunyayla in central Turkey, then to Syria
      <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 9" />.
    </p>

    <h3 id="mansura">Mansura</h3>
    <p>
      Kazzaz gives these household counts for Mansura
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 36, Table 3.1" />. The 1885 figure is from the
      Ottoman provincial yearbook, and the 1935 figure from ORSAM’s table of the
      French mandate census, which has the same number
      <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" />.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Year</th><th>Households</th><th>Source</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1885</td><td>184</td><td>Ottoman provincial yearbook (<i>Salname-i Vilayet-i Suriye</i> 1302/1885, p. 234), via <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 36" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>1935</td><td>120</td><td>French mandate census, in <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" /> and <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 36" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Kazzaz also gives the population of Mansura as 400, among the leading
      Circassian villages of the district <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 35" />, and
      records events there. In January 1895 Muslim inhabitants of the Shuf (in
      Lebanon) petitioned that its governor had incited the Druze of Majdal
      Shams to burn Circassian houses in Mansura
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 59" />, and in December 1895 the Circassians of
      Mansura beat back a Druze attack <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 60" />. In
      1903 the chief of the Circassians of Mansura, Mehmed Rashid, petitioned
      the government that the Fadl tribe had taken their fields, and that 64
      Circassians had been killed
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 64, note 238" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: that 1903 petition says the Circassians had held their fields
        for “twenty-seven years”, which points to about 1876, before the 1878
        wave <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 64, note 238" />. It is one party’s claim
        in a dispute, so it may be rounded or exaggerated. But it could mean
        some Mansura families came earlier than the main wave. I have not seen
        it explained in any of the six studies (my reading).
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="family">
    <h2>Amin Samkough’s grandfather and the Balkan route</h2>
    <p>
      The grandfather’s account comes from one Turkish biography of Amin
      Samkough. The same text appears on three sites:
      <a href={`${research}/bilgiler-emin-semsug/`}>Bilgiler</a>
      (<time datetime="2009-12-08">8 December 2009</time>),
      <a href={`${research}/circassian-center-emin-semsug/`}>Circassian Center</a>
      (2019) and
      <a href={`${research}/biyografi-net-emin-semsug/`}>Biyografi.Net</a>
      (undated, archived by 2025)
      <Cite page="bilgiler-emin-semsug" label="Bilgiler" at="same-text section" />.
      The shortened version on
      <a href={`${research}/yeni-akit-emin-semsug/`}>Yeni Akit</a>
      (27 October 2016) leaves the grandfather and father out
      <Cite page="yeni-akit-emin-semsug" label="Yeni Akit" at="review notes" />.
      Because the three copies share one text, they count as <strong>one
      source</strong>, not three.
    </p>
    <p>The sentence in the original, with my translation:</p>
    <blockquote lang="tr">
      Büyük Çerkes sürgününde (1864) önce Balkanlar’a, sonra da Suriye’ye göç
      etmek zorunda kalan dedesi, Mansure köyünün ileri gelenlerinden biri ve
      köy muhtarıydı. Babası Semguğ Eyüp de Trablusgarb’ı (Libya) İtalyan
      saldırısına karşı savunan Osmanlı güçlerine katılarak orada savaşmıştı
      (1911).
      <br />
      “His grandfather, who had to emigrate first to the Balkans and then to
      Syria during the Great Circassian exile (1864), was one of the notables
      of the village of Mansure and its headman (<span lang="tr">muhtar</span>).
      His father, Eyüp Semguğ, had also fought there, joining the Ottoman forces
      defending Tripolitania (Libya) against the Italian attack (1911).”
      <Cite page="circassian-center-emin-semsug" label="Circassian Center" />
      <a href="https://www.bilgiler.gen.tr/emin-semsug.html">original on Bilgiler</a>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The claims in it, one by one, set against the documented record:
    </p>
    <ul class="claims">
      <li>
        <strong>Claim 1. The grandfather moved first to the Balkans, then to
        Syria, in the exile of 1864.</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <em>What the biography says:</em> the sentence above. Note that the
            year “(1864)” is attached to the “Great Circassian exile”, not
            clearly to the grandfather’s own departure
            <Cite page="circassian-center-emin-semsug" label="Circassian Center" />.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>What the studies say:</em> 1864 is the year Russia ended the
            Caucasian wars <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 5" /> and the peak year of
            migration to the Ottoman Empire <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 8" />.
            Many Circassians were settled in the Balkans in the 1860s
            <Cite src="chochiev" at="pp. 202&ndash;203" />
            <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 1" />, and the main move to Syria came
            after 1878 <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 9" />
            <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 9" />. So the order “Balkans, then Syria”
            matches the documented route.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Other routes also existed:</em> small groups reached the Golan
            in the early 1870s <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 9" />, ORSAM’s elders
            describe a route through Samsun and Uzunyayla
            <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 9" />, and later groups came from Anatolia
            <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 31" />. A Balkan route is therefore
            consistent with the record, but is not the only one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <mark>
              Review: the biographies give no source, and do not name the
              grandfather. Since 1864 is the usual date of “the exile”, it may
              stand for the exile in general, not for a year this family moved
              (my reading).
            </mark>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claim 2. The grandfather was a notable and the headman
        (muhtar) of the village of Mansure.</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <em>What the biography says:</em> the sentence above, with no
            source <Cite page="circassian-center-emin-semsug" label="Circassian Center" />.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>What the studies say:</em> nothing about him. The one named
            leader of Mansura’s Circassians that Kazzaz records is Mehmed
            Rashid, in a petition of 9 March 1903
            <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 64, note 238" />. That is long after the
            1870s and 1880s, and the thesis says nothing about earlier headmen.
          </li>
          <li>
            <mark>
              Review: unsourced. Nothing in the six studies confirms or
              contradicts it.
            </mark>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claim 3. Amin Samkough was born in Mansure (Mansura) in the
        Golan.</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <em>Who says so:</em>
            the Turkish biography above (1900)
            <Cite page="circassian-center-emin-semsug" label="Circassian Center" />;
            eSyria (1900, al-Mansura)
            <Cite page="esyria-first-mayor-of-quneitra" label="eSyria" />;
            Arabic Wikipedia (1903, al-Mansura)
            <Cite page="arabic-wikipedia-amin-ayyub-samkoug" label="Arabic Wikipedia" />;
            and the Circassia Times blog post (1903, al-Mansura)
            <Cite page="circassia-times-amin-ayyub-samkoug" label="Circassia Times" />.
            The Russian dictionary entry gives “1900, Quneitra”, which names
            the district, not the village
            <Cite page="north-caucasians-in-emigration" label="dictionary entry" />.
            Mansura lay in the Quneitra district
            <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 35" />, so the two are not in conflict.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Do the places fit?</em> Mansura was a Circassian village in the
            Quneitra district, with 184 households in 1885
            <Cite src="kazzaz" at="pp. 31, 36" />, and Lash lists it among the
            Golan villages <Cite src="lash" at="web page" />. So the place is
            consistent with the history.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>The year is disputed.</em> 1900 appears in the Turkish
            biography, eSyria and the dictionary entry. 1903 appears in Arabic
            Wikipedia and Circassia Times, and in the caption of a portrait on
            this site’s <a href="/people/amin-samkough/images/">images page</a>.
            The biography page on this site gives 1900&ndash;1953
            <Cite page="/people/amin-samkough/biography/" label="biography page" />.
            The Arabic Wikipedia entry cites the eSyria article, which says
            1900
            <Cite page="arabic-wikipedia-amin-ayyub-samkoug" label="Arabic Wikipedia" at="note 1" />,
            and the Circassia Times post says 1903 for what its own page notes
            is the same eSyria text
            <Cite page="circassia-times-amin-ayyub-samkoug" label="Circassia Times" at="review notes" />.
          </li>
          <li>
            <mark>
              Review: the birthplace is consistent with the history, but the
              pages repeat one another. The Turkish pages share one text, and
              the Arabic pages draw on the eSyria article, so they are not
              independent confirmations. The birth year is still open.
            </mark>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claim 4. His father, Eyüp (Ayyub), fought in Libya in 1911,
        and died in Tripoli.</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <em>Who says so:</em> the Turkish biography says only that the
            father fought there (1911)
            <Cite page="circassian-center-emin-semsug" label="Circassian Center" />.
            eSyria says he died in Tripoli, Libya, defending it against the
            Italians, which is why his son had a free Ottoman scholarship
            <Cite page="esyria-first-mayor-of-quneitra" label="eSyria" />.
            Arabic Wikipedia and Circassia Times say he was killed in Tripoli in
            1911
            <Cite page="arabic-wikipedia-amin-ayyub-samkoug" label="Arabic Wikipedia" />
            <Cite page="circassia-times-amin-ayyub-samkoug" label="Circassia Times" />.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>What the studies say:</em> none of the six mentions Libya. It
            falls outside the migration they cover.
          </li>
          <li>
            <mark>
              Review: unsourced, and the sources differ on whether he fought
              or died there. Not tested against any study.
            </mark>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In short, the grandfather’s account is <strong>consistent with</strong>
      the documented route, but the documents used here do not mention him or
      his family. The place that links the two is Mansura.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        My suggestion, not from a source: to go further, a researcher would need
        records that list the family by name. Kazzaz draws on Ottoman provincial
        yearbooks and Ottoman archive files (for example, 1885 household counts
        per village
        <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 36" />), and ORSAM reproduces the French
        census of 1935 (also counted per village
        <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" />). Whether those or the Ottoman
        population registers name individual families is something I have not
        checked.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="numbers">
    <h2>How many came? The sources disagree</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Figure</th><th>Source</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>About 25,000 at the ports, mid-Feb to mid-Apr 1878</td>
          <td><Cite src="chochiev" at="p. 203" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>26,182 at the ports, Feb to Sep 1878 (but see the review note)</td>
          <td>
            <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 7" />,
            <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 13" />, both from Berzeg
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>45,000 at the ports by September 1878</td>
          <td><Cite src="orsam" at="p. 10" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>45,000 in Syria, Lebanon and “Libya” by the end of 1878</td>
          <td><Cite src="mirzoeva" at="web text" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>40,000&ndash;50,000 settled in Syria, 1878&ndash;80</td>
          <td><Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" />, from Russian consulates</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>About 70,000 settled in Syria, 1878&ndash;80</td>
          <td><Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" />, giving İzzet Aydemir’s estimate</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>50,000&ndash;60,000 in the province of Syria in total</td>
          <td><Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 13" />, from Berzeg</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>No more than 35,000&ndash;40,000 in Syria in the Ottoman period</td>
          <td><Cite src="lash" at="closing paragraph" /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The gap is wide because, as ORSAM says, the authorities did not record
      accurate numbers in 1878&ndash;80, and many people died
      <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" />.
      <mark>
        Review: Akbulat gives 70,000 as the total “together with those who
        arrived before 1878” and cites ORSAM <Cite src="akbulat" at="p. 7" />.
        The ORSAM report I read gives 70,000 only as Aydemir’s figure for
        1878&ndash;80 alone <Cite src="orsam" at="p. 15" />. The two are not the
        same claim.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="maps">
    <h2>Maps from the sources</h2>
    <p>
      Two of the six sources have maps: the Sabancı thesis (Kazzaz) and the
      Lash article. The other four have none that I could find
      <Cite src="akbulat" label="Akbulat" at="whole article" />,
      <Cite src="chochiev" at="whole article" />,
      <Cite src="mirzoeva" at="web text" /> and
      <Cite src="orsam" at="photos and tables only" />. All the maps below show
      where Circassian villages lay <em>within Syria</em> (or, for Lash’s
      second map, in Anatolia). <strong>None of them shows the earlier stages
      of the route, from the Caucasus or through the Balkans.</strong> Those
      stages are described in the text only. The images are shown as published
      in each source, with credit. The captions and the comments on what each
      map shows are mine, unless a citation is given.
    </p>

    <figure id="map-kazzaz">
      <a href="/migration/kazzaz-figure-3-1.jpg">
        <img
          src="/migration/kazzaz-figure-3-1.jpg"
          width="1193"
          height="615"
          alt="Period map of Syria and Lebanon with orange dots marking Circassian villages around Hama and Homs, around Damascus and Mount Hermon, and in the south around the Hauran, Golan and Balqa."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        <strong>Figure 3.1, “The Settlement Pattern of Circassian Villages in
        Greater Syria”.</strong> From Kazzaz’s thesis
        <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 26" />. The thesis credits it to Lewis,
        <em>Nomads and Settlers</em>, p. 100, and Aydemir, <em>Göç: Kuzey
        Kafkasyalıların Göç Tarihi</em>, p. 144. I cannot tell from the thesis
        how much of the map is from each.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>What it shows (my reading of the image).</strong> A period map
      with orange dots for Circassian villages. The dots cluster in three
      areas: around Hama and Homs, around Damascus and Mount Hermon, and in the
      south, in the Hauran, Golan and Balqa. The dots carry no labels, so I
      cannot pick out Mansura on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Context in the thesis.</strong> The figure ends section 3.1 of
      Kazzaz’s chapter 3. There he writes that “in Jawlan and
      Transjordan the pattern of Circassian settlements formed a barrier along
      the transitional zone”, with one main aim: to separate the populated,
      fertile coastal parts from semi-independent barren lands
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 25" />. He calls Quneitra “an ideal
      district” for settling refugees, because the land was fertile but used
      only by the Fadl tribe and others for grazing, it lay near the Druze at
      Majdal Shams, and it was close to Damascus
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 25" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thesis has one other picture in this chapter, Figure 3.2. It is a
      photograph of the Circassian village of Bireyka, built on ancient ruins,
      not a map
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 35" />.
    </p>

    <figure id="map-schumacher">
      <a href="/migration/schumacher-jawlan-1888.jpg">
        <img
          src="/migration/schumacher-jawlan-1888.jpg"
          width="1400"
          height="2203"
          alt="Black-and-white survey map of the Jaulân (Golan), with the title Map of the Jaulân by Gottlieb Schumacher, C.E. 1885, and place names in English transliteration."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        <strong>“Map of the Jaulân” by Gottlieb Schumacher</strong>, as
        reproduced in Kazzaz’s Appendix 10, which labels it “Schumacher’s map of
        Jawlan, 1888” <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 102" />. The map’s own title
        block says “C.E. 1885” and it carries the heading of the Palestine
        Exploration Fund. 1888 is the year of the book it comes from
        (<em>The Jaulân</em>, London: Richard Bentley and Son, 1888)
        <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 24, note 90" />.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>What it shows (my reading of the image).</strong> A detailed
      survey map of the whole Golan, with relief, roads and village names. At
      this size I can read <em>el Mansûrah</em> and <em>el Kunêtrah</em>
      (Quneitra) among the labels, with Mansura lying north of Quneitra and
      near the Roman road. It is a low-resolution scan of a large map, so please
      check the village positions against the original.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Context in the thesis.</strong> Schumacher’s survey is the main
      field source for Kazzaz’s chapter 3
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 6" />. His expedition “depicts the
      Circassian villages in great detail”, and he reports that Quneitra
      town had about 1,300 inhabitants, mostly Circassians
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 34" />. The same survey is the source for the
      Mansura population of 400 <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 35" />. It dates from
      the years of the 1885 yearbook count of 184 households for Mansura
      <Cite src="kazzaz" at="p. 36, Table 3.1" />.
    </p>

    <figure id="map-lash-syria">
      <a href="/migration/lash-circassian-settlements-1878-1967.jpg">
        <img
          src="/migration/lash-circassian-settlements-1878-1967.jpg"
          width="827"
          height="622"
          alt="Hand-annotated black-and-white map of Syria with red lines from the Golan, Damascus, Hama and Homs areas to boxed lists of village names in Arabic. A German caption at the lower right reads Ursprüngliche Tscherkessische Siedlungen in Syrien (1878–1967)."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        <strong>“Original Circassian settlements in Syria (1878&ndash;1967)”.</strong>
        The German caption on the map itself. The page’s caption, in Arabic:
        <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">خريطة أولية لتوزع التجمعات السكانية الشركسية في سورية بين سنة 1878 وسنة تهجيرهم من الجولان 1967</span>
        (“A preliminary map of the distribution of the Circassian settlements
        in Syria between 1878 and their displacement from the Golan in 1967”;
        my translation). <Cite src="lash" at="first map" />
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>What it shows (my reading of the image).</strong> A
      hand-annotated outline map of Syria. Red lines run from clusters of dots
      to boxed lists of village names in Arabic. One box, headed
      <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">قرى الجولان</span> (“villages of the Golan”), lists
      <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">المنصورة</span> (al-Mansura) <strong>first</strong>,
      then Quneitra and about ten more villages, such as Ayn Zivan,
      al-Mumsiya, Bir Ajam, al-Buraiqa, al-Juwayza, al-Fazara and al-Faraj;
      <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">رويحينة</span> (Ruhayna) is written apart from the box.
      I read this off an enlarged copy of a small image of handwriting, so
      some names may be misread. Other boxes list villages around Hama, Homs
      and Damascus. The map’s own Arabic title, at the lower right, adds Lebanon
      and Palestine to Syria.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Context in the article.</strong> The maps appear at the end of
      Lash’s article, after a footnote that names his earlier study, “The
      displacement of the Circassians to Syria and their settlement in the
      Circassian limes strip” (<span lang="ar" dir="rtl">تهجير الشركس إلى سورية وتوطينهم في شريط الليمس الشركسي</span>),
      published on the same site in January 2018
      <Cite src="lash" at="footnote 1" />. The page does not say who drew the
      maps, so they may come from that earlier study (my inference). The
      article’s own text says the largest Circassian concentration in Syria
      was in the Golan, and lists the Golan villages, Mansura among them
      <Cite src="lash" at="section on where Circassians lived in Syria, 1878&ndash;1967" />.
      It counts about 40 villages and one town in all
      <Cite src="lash" at="same section" />.
    </p>

    <figure id="map-lash-anatolia">
      <a href="/migration/lash-circassian-villages-anatolia.jpg">
        <img
          src="/migration/lash-circassian-villages-anatolia.jpg"
          width="609"
          height="409"
          alt="Low-quality photograph of a map of northern and central Turkey from the Black Sea coast to the south, with small coloured dots marking Circassian villages and a partial legend at the top left."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        <strong>Circassian villages in Anatolia.</strong> The page’s Arabic
        caption:
        <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">خريطة لجزء كبير من تركيا تبين انتشار القرى الشركسية في وسطها على امتداد شريط شمالي/ جنوبي يمتد من البحر الأسود شمالاً إلى سورية جنوبا ويكمل شريط الليمس الشركسي في سورية</span>
        (“A map of a large part of Turkey showing the spread of Circassian
        villages in its centre along a north–south strip running from the Black
        Sea in the north to Syria in the south, and completing the Circassian
        limes strip in Syria”; my translation). <Cite src="lash" at="second map" />
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong>What it shows (my reading of the image).</strong> A photograph of
      a map of northern and central Turkey, from the Black Sea coast down to the
      south, with small coloured dots for villages and part of a legend (partly
      legible, with entries such as Chechen and East Circassian) cut off at the top left. The
      image is of poor quality and the village names are not legible.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Context in the article.</strong> The caption ties it to Lash’s
      “limes” idea: that the Ottomans settled Circassians mainly in a strip
      between the cultivated land and the desert, and that this strip ran on
      from Anatolia into Syria. The article says Circassians were settled
      first in villages on either side of that strip, with the Golan as an
      exception, where the Ottomans seem to have wanted a human barrier against
      the Druze villages on Mount Hermon
      <Cite src="lash" at="text on the settlement strip" />. The map does not
      show the Balkans.
    </p>
  </section>

